+
+## Temporal frames supplied by an embedding host
+
+`filmcraft.provideFrames({info, read})` returns `{path, handle}`. The host uses
+that virtual file path in an ordinary media project item. `info` is MediaInfo
+for a positive-duration RGBA8 video without audio, at most 33,554,432 pixels and 8192 per side.
+`read(ticks)` receives source time as an exact decimal integer string
+(254016000000 ticks per second) and returns a Promise of straight RGBA8 bytes
+in a Uint8Array from the FilmCraft window's realm. Each response must contain
+exactly width * height * 4 bytes. Alpha is preserved.
+
+The source uses the existing asynchronous media pending/retry mechanism for
+preview and export. It permits one outstanding read per source, 64 sources,
+and a 32 MiB / 64-frame cache per source (at least one frame). Reads expire after 15 seconds.
+`frameSources()` reports handles, read counts, cache sizes and latched errors.
+`closeFrames(handle)` releases the callback and cache; late replies are ignored.
+The host owns persistent bindings, authorization and reconnection after reload.
+Virtual paths and handles alone cannot restore a saved source.
+
+During web playback, an unfinished asynchronous composite remains queued until
+all its layers are ready. The next request then catches up to the playback clock;
+completed pictures can be displayed even when more than six frames late. Layers
+still use the same exact source time. Stop requests the exact playhead picture;
+view, revision and draft changes discard incompatible pending work. This avoids
+starvation under source latency, but does not guarantee real-time frame rates.
+`apps/filmcraft-web/tests/host-frame-playback.html` exercises the 64-source limit and ten synthetic
+sources (one delayed by 600 ms), forward/reverse playback, Stop and seeks.
+
+On a failed read, incomplete frames remain pending, including during export,
+so the compositor cannot silently export its missing-media placeholder. The
+embedding host must surface `frameSources().error`, cancel affected exports
+through `jobs.cancel`, and reconnect the source. ArtCraft performs this check.
+The host must not close sources still used by an active export.
+
+All-Dings keeps Channel graphs, RCZ decoding, canonical measurement time and
+PNG decoding in its adapter. FilmCraft only sees its ordinary media interface;
+its project schema, timeline and compositor are unchanged.
